{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 199 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "56d83ba0740905563fcb110ddbf9c8df9b256895102858f02e2c4dd87f363853" :edb/db-before-t 198 :edb/db-after-t 199 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533511 50 #inst "2026-10-11T05:19:24.597Z" 13194139533511 17592186045585 true] [17592186171735 1071 "Suppose $n=4m^2+2m-1$, where $m \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. Construct an argument that explains why $n$ is odd." 13194139533511 17592186045585 false] [17592186171735 1071 "Suppose $n=4m^2+2m-1,$ where $m \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$ Construct an argument that explains why $n$ is odd." 13194139533511 17592186045585 true] [17592186171735 1074 "An integer is *odd* if it can be written as $2a + 1$, where $a$ is an integer.\n\nNow, note the following regarding the expression $4m^2+2m-1{:}$\n\n- All terms, apart from the constant term, have a common factor of $2$.\n- The constant term can be written as $-1 = -2+1$.\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\n> The number $n$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{odd}}$ since\n> $n|= 4m^{2} + 2m - 1; = (4m^{2} + 2m - 2) + 1; = 2(2 m 2 + m - 1_(⏟)_(a)) + 1; = 2 a + 1$,\n> where $a=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2m^2+m-1}$ is an integer." 13194139533511 17592186045585 false] [17592186171735 1074 "An integer is *odd* if it can be written as $2a + 1,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nNow, note the following regarding the expression $4m^2+2m-1{:}$\n\n- All terms, apart from the constant term, have a common factor of $2.$\n\n- The constant term can be written as $-1 = -2+1.$\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{odd}}$ since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =4{m}^{2}+2m-1 \\\\  & =(4{m}^{2}+2m-2)+1 \\\\  & =2({{2{m}^{2}+m-1}_{⏟}}_{a})+1 \\\\  & =2a+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2m^2+m-1}$ is an integer." 13194139533511 17592186045585 true] [17592186171736 1071 "Suppose $n=2pq - 4q + 5$, where $p,q \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. Construct an argument that explains why $n$ is odd." 13194139533511 17592186045585 false] [17592186171736 1071 "Suppose $n=2pq - 4q + 5,$ where $p,q \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$ Construct an argument that explains why $n$ is odd." 13194139533511 17592186045585 true] [17592186171736 1074 "An integer is *odd* if it can be written as $2a + 1$, where $a$ is an integer.\n\nNow, note the following regarding the expression $2pq - 4q + 5 {:}$\n\n- All terms, apart from the constant term, have a common factor of $2$.\n- The constant term can be written as $5 = 4+1$.\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\n> The number $n$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{odd}}$ since\n> $n|= 2pq - 4q + 5; = (2pq - 4q + 4) + 1; = 2(p q - 2 q + 2_(⏟)_(a)) + 1; = 2 a + 1$,\n> where $a= \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue} pq - 2q + 2\\in \\mathbb Z}$." 13194139533511 17592186045585 false] [17592186171736 1074 "An integer is *odd* if it can be written as $2a + 1,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nNow, note the following regarding the expression $2pq - 4q + 5 {:}$\n\n- All terms, apart from the constant term, have a common factor of $2.$\n\n- The constant term can be written as $5 = 4+1.$\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm{odd}}$ since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =2pq-4q+5 \\\\  & =(2pq-4q+4)+1 \\\\  & =2({{pq-2q+2}_{⏟}}_{a})+1 \\\\  & =2a+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $a= \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue} pq - 2q + 2\\in \\mathbb Z }.$" 13194139533511 17592186045585 true] [17592186171737 1071 "Suppose $n=10-30p$, where $p \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. Construct an argument that explains why $n$ is even." 13194139533511 17592186045585 false] [17592186171737 1071 "Suppose $n=10-30p,$ where $p \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$ Construct an argument that explains why $n$ is even." 13194139533511 17592186045585 true] [17592186171737 1074 "An integer is *even* if it can be written as $2a$, where $a$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\n> The number $n$ is even since\n> $n|= 10 - 30p; = 2(5 - 15 p_(⏟)_(a)); = 2a$,\n> where we have defined $a=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}5-15p}$, which is an integer." 13194139533511 17592186045585 false] [17592186171737 1074 "An integer is *even* if it can be written as $2a,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is even since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =10-30p \\\\  & =2({{5-15p}_{⏟}}_{a}) \\\\  & =2a,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have defined $a=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}5-15p},$ which is an integer." 13194139533511 17592186045585 true] [17592186171738 1071 "Suppose $n=3-4m$, where $m \\in \\mathbb{Z}$. Construct an argument that explains why $n$ is odd." 13194139533511 17592186045585 false] [17592186171738 1071 "Suppose $n=3-4m,$ where $m \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$ Construct an argument that explains why $n$ is odd." 13194139533511 17592186045585 true] [17592186171738 1074 "An integer is *odd* if it can be written as $2a + 1$, where $a$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\n> The number $n$ is *odd* since\n> $n|= 3 - 4m; = (-4m + 2) + 1; = 2(-2 m + 1_(⏟)_(a)) + 1; = 2a + 1$,\n> where we have defined $a=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-2m+1}$, which is an integer." 13194139533511 17592186045585 false] [17592186171738 1074 "An integer is *odd* if it can be written as $2a + 1,$ where $a$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we complete our argument as follows:\n\nThe number $n$ is *odd* since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n & =3-4m \\\\  & =(-4m+2)+1 \\\\  & =2({{-2m+1}_{⏟}}_{a})+1 \\\\  & =2a+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we have defined $a=\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-2m+1},$ which is an integer." 13194139533511 17592186045585 true] [17592186171931 1038 #edb/ref 17592186406662 13194139533511 17592186045585 true] [17592186171931 1038 #edb/ref 17592186406663 13194139533511 17592186045585 true] [17592186171931 1038 #edb/ref 17592186406664 13194139533511 17592186045585 true] [17592186171931 1038 #edb/ref 17592186406665 13194139533511 17592186045585 true] [17592186173279 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to prove universal statements that involve the evenness and oddness of simple expressions.\n\nFirst, we have the following definitions:\n\n- Two integers have the **same parity** if they're *both* even or *both* odd.\n- Two integers have **opposite parity** if *one* of them is even and the *other* is odd.\n\nFor example:\n\n- The integers $2$ and $4$ have the *same* parity because they're both even.\n- The integers $1$ and $7$ have the *same* parity because they're both odd.\n- On the other hand, the integers $1$ and $6$ have *opposite* parity because $1$ is odd and $6$ is even." 13194139533511 17592186045585 false] [17592186173279 1064 "In this lesson, we'll learn how to prove universal statements that involve the evenness and oddness of simple expressions. \n\nFirst, we have the following definitions:\n\n- Two integers have the **same parity** if they're *both* even or *both* odd.\n\n- Two integers have **opposite parity** if *one* of them is even and the *other* is odd.\n\nFor example:\n\n- The integers $2$ and $4$ have the *same* parity because they're both even.\n\n- The integers $1$ and $7$ have the *same* parity because they're both odd.\n\n- On the other hand, the integers $1$ and $6$ have *opposite* parity because $1$ is odd and $6$ is even." 13194139533511 17592186045585 true] [17592186173280 1064 "Recall that an integer is *odd* if it is not even. The list below gives the first few odd integers:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1, \\quad \\pm 3, \\quad \\pm 5, \\quad \\pm 7, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nNotice that every odd integer can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{2}}\\times {\\color{blue}{\\textrm{some other integer}}} + 1\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= 2 \\cdot 0 + 1 \\\\\n3 &= 2 \\cdot 1 + 1 \\\\\n5 &= 2 \\cdot 2 + 1 \\\\\n19 &= 2 \\cdot 9 + 1 \\\\\n- 17 &= 2 \\cdot (-9) + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, an integer $n$ is odd if it can be written as\n\n$$\nn={\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}+1\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}a$ is an integer." 13194139533511 17592186045585 false] [17592186173280 1064 "Recall that an integer is *odd* if it is not even. The list below gives the first few odd integers:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1, \\quad \\pm 3, \\quad \\pm 5, \\quad \\pm 7, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nNotice that every odd integer can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{2}}\\times {\\color{blue}{\\textrm{some other integer}}} + 1.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =2\\times 0+1 \\\\ 3 & =2\\times 1+1 \\\\ 5 & =2\\times 2+1 \\\\ 19 & =2\\times 9+1 \\\\ -17 & =2\\times (-9)+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn general, an integer $n$ is odd if it can be written as \n\n$$\nn={\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}+1\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}a$ is an integer." 13194139533511 17592186045585 true] [17592186173292 1064 "Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ $P:\\: \\forall n\\in\\mathbb Z,\\, 8n-4$ *is even.*\n\nTo prove this statement, we need to show that $8n-4$ can be written as $2{\\color{blue}{a}}$, where ${\\color{blue}{a}}$ is an integer.\n\nThe first step is to factor out $2$ from the expression.\n\n$$\n8n-4 = 2(4n-2)\n$$\n\nNext, we introduce a new variable ${\\color{blue}{a}}$, where\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{a}} = 4n-2\n$$\n\nNotice that since $n\\in\\mathbb Z$, we have that $4n-2\\in\\mathbb Z$. In other words, ${\\color{blue}{a}}\\in\\mathbb Z$. It's essential to state this since the whole argument breaks down if the second factor is sometimes not an integer.\n\nNow, our expression can be written as\n\n$$\n2(4n-2) = 2{\\color{blue}{a}}\n$$\n\nSo, since $8n-4$ can be written in the form $2{\\color{blue}{a}}$ where ${\\color{blue}{a}}$ is an integer, we conclude that $8n-4$ is even *for all* $n\\in\\mathbb Z$.\n\nLet's take a look at another example. Before we do, note that statements about parity are often written informally, and so the universal quantifier $\\forall$ is usually hidden." 13194139533511 17592186045585 false] [17592186173292 1064 "Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ $P:\\: \\forall n\\in\\mathbb Z,\\, 8n-4$ *is even.*\n\nTo prove this statement, we need to show that $8n-4$ can be written as $2{\\color{blue}{a}},$ where ${\\color{blue}{a}}$ is an integer. \n\nThe first step is to factor out $2$ from the expression.\n\n$$\n8n-4 = 2(4n-2)\n$$\n\nNext, we introduce a new variable ${\\color{blue}{a}},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{a}} = 4n-2.\n$$\n\nNotice that since $n\\in\\mathbb Z,$ we have that $4n-2\\in\\mathbb Z.$ In other words, ${\\color{blue}{a}}\\in\\mathbb Z.$ It's essential to state this since the whole argument breaks down if the second factor is sometimes not an integer. \n\nNow, our expression can be written as\n\n$$\n2(4n-2) = 2{\\color{blue}{a}}.\n$$\n\nSo, since $8n-4$ can be written in the form $2{\\color{blue}{a}}$ where ${\\color{blue}{a}}$ is an integer, we conclude that $8n-4$ is even *for all* $n\\in\\mathbb Z.$\n\nLet's take a look at another example. Before we do, note that statements about parity are often written informally, and so the universal quantifier $\\forall$ is usually hidden." 13194139533511 17592186045585 true] [17592186173293 1064 "The list below gives the first few even integers:\n\n$$\n0, \\quad \\pm 2, \\quad \\pm 4, \\quad \\pm 6, \\quad \\pm 8, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nAn integer is even if it is a multiple of $2$. And, since every integer is a multiple of its factors, every even integer can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{2}}\\times {\\color{blue}{\\textrm{some other integer}}}\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n0 &= 2 \\cdot 0 \\\\\n2 &= 2 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n4 &= 2 \\cdot 2 \\\\\n12 &= 2 \\cdot 6 \\\\\n- 24 &= 2 \\cdot (-12)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSuppose we know that an integer $n$ is even. Then, we can rewrite $n$ as follows:\n\n$$\nn={\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{blue}{a}}$ is an integer.\n\nThis choice of letter for the second factor is arbitrary: we chose ${\\color{blue}{a}}$ here, but we can use any letter we like! However, the following variables are commonly used to denote integers:\n$a, \\quad b, \\quad c, \\quad k, \\quad l, \\quad m, \\quad n, \\quad p, \\quad q, \\quad r, \\quad x, \\quad y, \\quad z$" 13194139533511 17592186045585 false] [17592186173293 1064 "The list below gives the first few even integers:\n\n$$\n0, \\quad \\pm 2, \\quad \\pm 4, \\quad \\pm 6, \\quad \\pm 8, \\quad \\ldots\n$$\n\nAn integer is even if it is a multiple of $2.$ And, since every integer is a multiple of its factors, every even integer can be written as \n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{2}}\\times {\\color{blue}{\\textrm{some other integer}}}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =2\\times 0 \\\\ 2 & =2\\times 1 \\\\ 4 & =2\\times 2 \\\\ 12 & =2\\times 6 \\\\ -24 & =2\\times (-12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSuppose we know that an integer $n$ is even. Then, we can rewrite $n$ as follows:\n\n$$\nn={\\color{red}{2}}{\\color{blue}{a}}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\color{blue}{a}}$ is an integer. \n\nThis choice of letter for the second factor is arbitrary: we chose ${\\color{blue}{a}}$ here, but we can use any letter we like! However, the following variables are commonly used to denote integers:\n\n$$\na, \\quad b, \\quad c, \\quad k, \\quad l, \\quad m, \\quad n, \\quad p, \\quad q, \\quad r, \\quad x, \\quad y, \\quad z\n$$" 13194139533511 17592186045585 true] [17592186173294 1064 "Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ $P:\\: \\forall n\\in\\mathbb Z,\\, 4n+7$ *is odd.*\n\nTo prove this statement, we need to show that it can be written as $2{\\color{blue}{a}}+1$, where ${\\color{blue}{a}}$ is an integer.\n\nThe first step is to rewrite the expression by isolating $+1$. We can do this by decomposing $7$ into $6+1$, as follows:\n\n$$\n4n+7 = 4n+6+1 = (4n+6)+1\n$$\n\nNext, we factor out $2$ from the expression in parentheses:\n\n$$\n(4n+6)+1 = 2(2n+3) + 1\n$$\n\nThen, we introduce a new variable ${\\color{blue}{a}}$, where\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{a}} = 2n+3\n$$\n\nNotice that since $n\\in\\mathbb Z$, we have that $2n+3\\in\\mathbb Z$. In other words, ${\\color{blue}{a}}\\in\\mathbb Z$.\n\nThis means our original expression can be written as\n\n$$\n2(2n+3) + 1= 2{\\color{blue}{a}} + 1\n$$\n\nSo, since $4n+7$ can be written in the form $2{\\color{blue}{a}}+1$ where ${\\color{blue}{a}}$ is an integer, we conclude that $4n+7$ is odd *for all* $n\\in\\mathbb Z$." 13194139533511 17592186045585 false] [17592186173294 1064 "Consider the following statement:\n\n$\\qquad$ $P:\\: \\forall n\\in\\mathbb Z,\\, 4n+7$ *is odd.*\n\nTo prove this statement, we need to show that it can be written as $2{\\color{blue}{a}}+1,$ where ${\\color{blue}{a}}$ is an integer. \n\nThe first step is to rewrite the expression by isolating $+1.$ We can do this by decomposing $7$ into $6+1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n4n+7 = 4n+6+1 = (4n+6)+1\n$$\n\nNext, we factor out $2$ from the expression in parentheses:\n\n$$\n(4n+6)+1 = 2(2n+3) + 1\n$$\n\nThen, we introduce a new variable ${\\color{blue}{a}},$ where\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{a}} = 2n+3.\n$$\n\nNotice that since $n\\in\\mathbb Z,$ we have that $2n+3\\in\\mathbb Z.$ In other words, ${\\color{blue}{a}}\\in\\mathbb Z.$ \n\nThis means our original expression can be written as\n\n$$\n2(2n+3) + 1= 2{\\color{blue}{a}} + 1.\n$$\n\nSo, since $4n+7$ can be written in the form $2{\\color{blue}{a}}+1$ where ${\\color{blue}{a}}$ is an integer, we conclude that $4n+7$ is odd *for all* $n\\in\\mathbb Z.$" 13194139533511 17592186045585 true] [17592186406614 1081 "12-15p" 13194139533511 17592186045585 true] [17592186406614 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533511 17592186045585 true] [17592186406614 1084 #uuid "026880e2-e551-5990-8bbd-db4f549c4604" 13194139533511 17592186045585 true] [17592186406615 1081 "2a-1" 13194139533511 17592186045585 true] [17592186406615 1083 #edb/ref 17592186045499 13194139533511 17592186045585 true] [17592186406615 1084 #uuid "05e50cf2-3b9e-5959-